# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This guide is for Integration Hub team members setting up and testing
[file collection through the portal](../supported-patterns/pull-from-presigned-url.html).
The pattern is a draft: check the configuration and complete live access
tests before offering it to a customer.

## Before you start

Agree the source identity and prefix, recipient group and Slack channel.
Confirm the group is already assigned to the Transfer web app in the target
environment. App access alone does not grant access to the pickup folder.

Check that the AWS account is connected to the appropriate Slack workspace
through Amazon Q Developer in chat applications. This connection requires a
one-time Slack authorisation; Terraform cannot complete it. Use an authorised
test environment and synthetic files for the checks below.

## Configure a recipient

1. Add the recipient to the relevant source prefix in
   [`modules/file-dispatch-configuration/locals-<environment>.tf`](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/tree/main/terraform/environments/integration-hub-file-transfer/modules/file-dispatch-configuration).
   Follow the module's Slack pickup configuration. Set `notifications.slack`
   to the same stable recipient ID as `pickup.recipient_id`, and provide the
   authorised group IDs, Slack channel ID and workspace ID in `pickup`.
   Preserve any existing action and other notification settings.
2. Review and apply a fresh plan for the root file-transfer configuration,
   then review and apply the `pull-from-presigned-url` component through the
   approved platform workflow. Check that each read grant is limited to the
   intended recipient directory.
3. Check the matching dispatch secret through the approved privileged access
   route. Terraform creates its initial value but ignores later changes. If
   the secret already exists, update it as needed so `notifications.slack`
   matches the recipient ID, preserving the rest of the configuration. Do not
   put secret values in source control, logs or tickets.
4. Upload a new synthetic file through the configured source. Existing events
   refer to their original dispatch-secret version, so changing the
   configuration does not update an earlier request.

The pickup component publishes to a recipient-specific SNS topic. Amazon Q
Developer in chat applications sends the notification to Slack; this setup
does not use an incoming webhook.

## Check the result

Confirm the copy exists and that the Slack message arrives in the intended
channel. The message gives a portal link, file name and URL-encoded pickup
path; it does not contain the bucket name or a direct file link. Sign in,
find the file and compare the download with the original synthetic file.
SNS accepting a notification does not by itself prove it reached Slack.

Before customer use, also check that:

* an authorised group member can find and download the file
* another web-app user cannot list or download it, even with the Slack link
* the pickup grant does not allow upload or deletion, or access to another
  recipient's directory without a separate grant
* a small file and an approved test file larger than 5 GB download intact
* a fresh download works after an old download request exceeds five minutes;
  investigate any portal or retry problems
* removing group access behaves as expected, including any delay for
  already-issued credentials
* quarantined files do not produce pickup notifications

The pickup bucket has a seven-day lifecycle rule. Removal is asynchronous and
does not enforce an exact access or deletion deadline. Agree any strict
retention requirements before adopting this pattern.

## If a notification is missing

Check the file reached the clean bucket and that the most-specific dispatch
configuration requested Slack pickup for the intended recipient. Then check
the notifier logs, processing queue, dead-letter queues, recipient SNS topic
and Slack integration. The notifier records `published` when SNS accepts the
message; check Slack separately.

Events more than 12 hours old are skipped. A retry normally reuses a saved
copy and does not extend its collection window, but an uncertain publication
can result in a duplicate notification. Do not assume a timed-out operation
had no effect. Preserve the evidence and escalate if the copy or publication
outcome is ambiguous; use the recovery procedure for the affected component
rather than replaying a message blindly.

## Architecture

![Overview of clean-file pickup: an event triggers a copy to a private pickup bucket and a Slack notification. Authorised users sign in to the Transfer web app to read their pickup directory.](/images/supported-patterns-pull-from-presigned-url/pull-from-presigned-url.png)

The diagram simplifies the notification path: after the copy, the notifier
publishes to SNS and Amazon Q Developer in chat applications delivers the
Slack message. The pickup component owns this copy and bucket; it does not use
the separate hosted S3 pickup executor.

